# Prototypal Inheritance & the Prototype Chain

## 1. What is it?

JavaScript objects can inherit properties and methods from other objects through a link called `[[Prototype]]` (exposed as `__proto__`, and accessible properly via `Object.getPrototypeOf()`/`Object.setPrototypeOf()`). When you access a property on an object and it isn't found directly on that object, the engine walks up this chain of linked objects — the **prototype chain** — until it finds the property or reaches `null`.

This is fundamentally different from classical (class-based) inheritance in languages like Java or C++, where classes are blueprints and objects are instances stamped from them. In JavaScript, **objects inherit directly from other objects** — there's no separate "class" concept at the engine level. `class` syntax (ES6+) is sugar on top of this same prototype mechanism.

## 2. Why introduced?

JavaScript was designed in 10 days by Brendan Eich, and he borrowed ideas from Self (a prototype-based language) rather than implementing classical inheritance like Java (which the language was originally meant to complement). The goals:

- **Simplicity/memory efficiency**: instead of every object carrying its own copy of every method, objects can share behavior by linking to a common prototype object. One `Array.prototype.push` function exists in memory, and every array just links to it.
- **Dynamism**: prototypes can be modified at runtime, and every object linked to that prototype instantly gets the change — useful for JS's dynamic, script-like nature.
- **Flexibility**: objects can be built up, composed, and re-linked at runtime without rigid class hierarchies — well suited to a language meant to run inside a browser and be written quickly.

## 3. How does it work?

**The chain mechanics:**

- Every object has an internal `[[Prototype]]` slot (accessible via `Object.getPrototypeOf(obj)`, historically via `obj.__proto__`).
- Every **function** (when used as a constructor) has a `.prototype` property — an ordinary object that becomes the `[[Prototype]]` of instances created with `new`.
- Property lookup: `obj.prop` → check `obj` own properties → not found? check `Object.getPrototypeOf(obj)` → not found? check that object's prototype → ... → until `Object.getPrototypeOf(x) === null` (the end of the chain, typically `Object.prototype`'s prototype).
- Property *writes* (`obj.prop = x`) do **not** walk the chain the same way — they create/set an **own** property directly on `obj` (unless the property is an inherited setter/getter, or non-writable up the chain, which can block the write in strict mode).

**`Object.create(proto, [propertiesObject])`:**

Creates a brand-new object whose `[[Prototype]]` is set directly to whatever you pass in — no constructor function, no `new`, no implicit prototype object involved.

```js
const animal = { speak() { console.log('...'); } };
const dog = Object.create(animal); // dog's [[Prototype]] IS animal, directly
```

This is the purest, most explicit way to set up prototypal inheritance — you're literally saying "this object's prototype is that object." `Object.create(null)` creates an object with **no** prototype at all (not even `Object.prototype`) — useful for a "dictionary" object immune to prototype pollution.

**Constructor functions:**

```js
function Dog(name) {
  this.name = name;
}
Dog.prototype.bark = () => console.log('Woof');

const rex = new Dog('Rex');
```

`new Dog('Rex')` does roughly:
1. Create a new plain object.
2. Set that object's `[[Prototype]]` to `Dog.prototype`.
3. Call `Dog` with `this` bound to the new object.
4. Return the new object (unless `Dog` explicitly returns another object).

So `rex.bark` isn't on `rex` itself — it's found by walking up to `Dog.prototype`.

**`class` syntax:**

```js
class Dog {
  constructor(name) { this.name = name; }
  bark() { console.log('Woof'); }
}
```

This compiles down to essentially the same constructor-function + `.prototype` mechanism above. `class` is **syntactic sugar** — under the hood, `bark` still lives on `Dog.prototype`, and `new Dog()` still does the same `[[Prototype]]` linking. The differences are enforced at the language level, not the underlying mechanism (see section 3b below).

**class vs constructor function — the real differences:**

| | Constructor function | `class` |
|---|---|---|
| Hoisting | Function declarations are hoisted and usable before definition | Classes are hoisted but land in the "temporal dead zone" — using before declaration throws |
| Calling without `new` | Silently runs with `this` as `undefined`/global object (non-strict) — a common bug source | Throws `TypeError: Class constructor cannot be invoked without 'new'` |
| Strict mode | Not automatic | Class bodies are always implicitly strict mode |
| Enumerability of methods | Methods added via `Function.prototype.method = ...` are enumerable by default (show up in `for...in`) | Methods defined in a class body are **non-enumerable** by default |
| Syntax for inheritance | Manual: `Child.prototype = Object.create(Parent.prototype)` + `Parent.call(this, ...)` | Declarative: `extends` + `super()` |
| Static members | Attached manually: `Dog.staticMethod = ...` | `static` keyword |

## 4. Example

```js
// --- Constructor function style ---
function Animal(name) {
  this.name = name;
}
Animal.prototype.speak = function () {
  console.log(`${this.name} makes a sound.`);
};

function Dog(name, breed) {
  Animal.call(this, name);       // (1) borrow Animal's constructor logic
  this.breed = breed;
}
Dog.prototype = Object.create(Animal.prototype); // (2) link Dog.prototype -> Animal.prototype
Dog.prototype.constructor = Dog;                 // (3) fix constructor reference
Dog.prototype.bark = function () {
  console.log(`${this.name} barks!`);
};

const rex = new Dog('Rex', 'Labrador');
rex.speak(); // "Rex makes a sound." (inherited from Animal.prototype)
rex.bark();  // "Rex barks!" (own on Dog.prototype)

console.log(rex.hasOwnProperty('name'));  // true  (own property)
console.log(rex.hasOwnProperty('speak')); // false (inherited)
console.log(Object.getPrototypeOf(rex) === Dog.prototype);            // true
console.log(Object.getPrototypeOf(Dog.prototype) === Animal.prototype); // true


// --- Equivalent class style ---
class AnimalC {
  constructor(name) { this.name = name; }
  speak() { console.log(`${this.name} makes a sound.`); }
}
class DogC extends AnimalC {
  constructor(name, breed) {
    super(name); // calls AnimalC's constructor
    this.breed = breed;
  }
  bark() { console.log(`${this.name} barks!`); }
}
const fido = new DogC('Fido', 'Poodle');
fido.speak(); // "Fido makes a sound."
fido.bark();  // "Fido barks!"


// --- Object.create style (no constructors at all) ---
const animalProto = {
  speak() { console.log(`${this.name} makes a sound.`); },
};
const dogProto = Object.create(animalProto);
dogProto.bark = function () { console.log(`${this.name} barks!`); };

const buddy = Object.create(dogProto);
buddy.name = 'Buddy';
buddy.speak(); // "Buddy makes a sound."
buddy.bark();  // "Buddy barks!"
```

## 5. Example Explanation — Execution Flow

For `rex.speak()`:

1. Engine looks for `speak` as an **own** property on `rex`. `rex` only has `name` and `breed` as own properties → not found.
2. Engine checks `Object.getPrototypeOf(rex)`, which is `Dog.prototype` (set up by step (2) in the constructor-function example, or automatically by `class ... extends`). `Dog.prototype` has `bark` and `constructor`, but not `speak` → not found.
3. Engine checks `Object.getPrototypeOf(Dog.prototype)`, which is `Animal.prototype` (again, linked in step (2)/`extends`). `Animal.prototype` has `speak` → **found**. It's invoked with `this` still bound to `rex` (that's why `this.name` correctly prints "Rex", not something on `Animal.prototype`).
4. If it hadn't been found there either, the engine would check `Object.getPrototypeOf(Animal.prototype)` (which is `Object.prototype`), and finally `Object.getPrototypeOf(Object.prototype)`, which is `null` → lookup ends, `undefined` is returned (or `TypeError` if you tried to call it).

For the `Object.create` version (`buddy.speak()`): same walk, just one link shorter conceptually since there's no constructor-function indirection — `buddy → dogProto → animalProto → Object.prototype → null`.

## 6. When to use?

- **`class` syntax**: the default choice for almost all modern application code — clearer syntax, built-in `extends`/`super`, safer semantics (throws instead of silently misbehaving), and what most codebases and linters expect.
- **`Object.create`**: when you want inheritance **without** a constructor function at all — e.g., building a prototype-based object literally from another object (factory patterns, `Object.create(null)` for a safe dictionary/map-like object immune to prototype pollution, or implementing the "prototypal OO" pattern popularized by Douglas Crockford).
- **Raw constructor functions**: rarely written by hand today except when you need to support very old environments without transpilation, or you're working inside a codebase/library predating ES6 classes. Understanding them is still essential because `class` compiles down to this exact mechanism, and interviewers use it to test whether you understand what's happening under the hood.

## 7. Advantages / Disadvantages

**Advantages of prototypal inheritance:**
- Memory-efficient: shared methods live once on the prototype, not duplicated per instance.
- Dynamic: you can extend/monkey-patch a prototype at runtime and every existing + future instance sees the change.
- Flexible: objects can inherit from other objects directly (`Object.create`) without needing a class hierarchy at all.

**Disadvantages / gotchas:**
- Mutating a shared prototype affects **all** instances — easy to introduce spooky-action-at-a-distance bugs, especially with built-in prototypes (`Array.prototype.foo = ...` is considered bad practice).
- The `[[Prototype]]` chain adds a lookup cost on every property access miss (usually negligible, but matters in hot loops).
- Manual constructor-function inheritance (`Object.create(Parent.prototype)` + `.call()`) is verbose and easy to get wrong (forgetting to reset `.constructor`, forgetting `Parent.call(this, ...)`).
- `class` hides the mechanism, which can make debugging prototype-chain issues (like unexpected inherited properties) harder for developers who don't understand what's underneath.

## 8. Common Interview Traps

- Saying JavaScript classes work "just like Java/C++ classes" — they don't; `class` is sugar over the same prototype-linking mechanism, not a separate inheritance model.
- Confusing `prototype` (a property that exists **only on functions**, pointing to the object that will become instances' `[[Prototype]]`) with `__proto__`/`[[Prototype]]` (a property that exists on **every object**, pointing to its own prototype link). `Dog.prototype` and `rex.__proto__` are usually the *same object*, but they are conceptually different properties on different things.
- Thinking `Object.create(proto)` calls a constructor — it doesn't; it just links the prototype. No `constructor` function runs.
- Forgetting that class methods are **non-enumerable**, unlike properties set via `SomeConstructor.prototype.method = fn`, which trips people up in `for...in` loop questions.
- Believing calling a class without `new` just "works differently" — it actually **throws** a `TypeError`, unlike old-style constructor functions which silently misbehave (`this` becomes `undefined` in strict mode, or the global object in sloppy mode).
- Confusing `hasOwnProperty` results — forgetting that inherited methods return `false` from `obj.hasOwnProperty('methodName')` even though `obj.methodName` works fine (because of the chain).
- Not knowing `Object.create(null)` exists — leads to overlooking the "objects without a prototype" trick for safe dictionaries immune to prototype pollution attacks.

## 9. Interview Follow-up Questions

**Q1: What's the difference between `Object.getPrototypeOf(obj)` and `obj.constructor.prototype`?**
`Object.getPrototypeOf(obj)` reliably returns the actual `[[Prototype]]` link, regardless of what `constructor` points to. `obj.constructor.prototype` relies on the `constructor` property being correctly set (which is a mutable, reassignable property on the prototype object) — if someone reassigns `Dog.prototype = {...}` without resetting `.constructor`, `obj.constructor` can lie about what actually constructed the object. `Object.getPrototypeOf` is the trustworthy source of truth.

**Q2: How does `instanceof` actually work?**
`obj instanceof Dog` checks whether `Dog.prototype` appears anywhere in `obj`'s prototype chain — it walks `Object.getPrototypeOf(obj)`, `Object.getPrototypeOf(Object.getPrototypeOf(obj))`, etc., comparing each link to `Dog.prototype`. It has nothing to do with `obj.constructor`.

**Q3: What happens if you set `Dog.prototype = Animal.prototype` directly instead of `Object.create(Animal.prototype)`?**
Now `Dog.prototype` and `Animal.prototype` are the literal same object — any method you add to `Dog.prototype` (like `bark`) also appears on `Animal.prototype`, leaking dog-specific behavior onto plain animals. `Object.create(Animal.prototype)` creates a **new** object that merely links to `Animal.prototype`, keeping the two separate while still inheriting.

**Q4: Can you change an object's prototype after creation?**
Yes, via `Object.setPrototypeOf(obj, newProto)` (or the legacy `obj.__proto__ = newProto`), but this is discouraged — it de-optimizes property access in most engines (V8 has to de-opt hidden classes/shapes) and is generally considered a code smell outside of polyfills/special tooling.

**Q5: What's the prototype chain's end point, and why does it matter?**
`Object.prototype`'s own `[[Prototype]]` is `null`. That's where lookups stop and return `undefined`. It matters because `Object.create(null)` skips linking to `Object.prototype` entirely, producing an object with **no** inherited methods at all (no `.toString`, `.hasOwnProperty`, etc.) — useful for pure data dictionaries immune to prototype-pollution style attacks, but it means you can't safely call `obj.hasOwnProperty(x)` — you'd need `Object.prototype.hasOwnProperty.call(obj, x)`.

**Q6: Are class fields (e.g., `class Dog { breed = 'unknown'; }`) on the prototype or the instance?**
Instance fields declared this way are set as **own properties** on each instance (assigned in the constructor, essentially), not on the prototype — unlike methods, which live on `Dog.prototype`. This is why two `Dog` instances have independent `breed` values but share the same `bark` function reference.

## 10. Interview-Ready Answer

"JavaScript uses prototypal inheritance: every object has an internal link — `[[Prototype]]` — to another object, and when you access a property that isn't found on the object itself, the engine walks up that chain until it finds it or hits `null`. Functions used as constructors have a `.prototype` property that becomes new instances' `[[Prototype]]` when you call them with `new`. `Object.create(proto)` is the most direct way to use this — it creates a new object whose prototype is exactly whatever you pass in, with no constructor involved at all. ES6 `class` syntax is sugar over this exact same mechanism — `extends` and `super` just automate the `Object.create(Parent.prototype)` + `Parent.call(this, ...)` dance you'd otherwise write by hand with constructor functions — but classes add real behavioral differences: they're not hoisted the same way (temporal dead zone), calling one without `new` throws instead of silently corrupting `this`, and their methods are non-enumerable by default. Understanding the underlying prototype chain matters because it explains *why* class-based code behaves the way it does, and it's essential for debugging things like unexpected inherited properties or `instanceof` checks."

## 11. Quick Revision

- Every object has a `[[Prototype]]` link; property lookup walks this chain until found or `null`.
- Functions have `.prototype` (becomes instances' `[[Prototype]]` via `new`); every object has `[[Prototype]]`/`__proto__` — don't confuse the two.
- `Object.create(proto)` directly sets an object's prototype — no constructor runs.
- `Object.create(null)` = object with zero inherited methods, immune to prototype pollution.
- `class` = syntactic sugar over constructor functions + `.prototype`, same mechanism under the hood.
- Real differences: classes are not usable before declaration (TDZ), throw without `new`, are auto-strict-mode, and their methods are non-enumerable.
- `hasOwnProperty` returns `false` for inherited properties even though property access still works.
- `instanceof` checks whether `Constructor.prototype` appears anywhere in the object's prototype chain.
- Avoid `Object.setPrototypeOf`/`__proto__` reassignment after creation — de-optimizes engines.

## 12. Memory Trick

**"Objects point up, not down."**
Picture a ladder: your object is at the bottom rung, and every rung above is a prototype. When you ask for a property, you start at your own rung and climb up rung by rung (`[[Prototype]]` → `[[Prototype]]` → ...) until someone answers or you fall off the top (`null`). `class` just builds that ladder for you automatically with `extends`/`super`; `Object.create(proto)` lets you nail a single rung in place yourself, by hand.
